namespace primerpicker::glossary {

// The 25 built-in definitions, in the order of glossary.md
inline constexpr auto glossary_table = std::array<Entry, 25>{{
    Entry{.key = "primer",
          .aliases = "",
          .title = R"glossary(Primer)glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** A short single-stranded DNA oligonucleotide that
anneals to a template and is extended at its 3' end by a DNA
polymerase. Written 5' to 3', with A, C, G and T.

**Method.** Primers have 2 to 50 bases (the 50-base limit is Primer
Designer's entry field). A primer taken from a molecule is identified
by the position of its 5' end on the top strand, and by its strand
(top or complement). Default length: 20 bases, 5' extension included.

*Source:* README "Primer Tm" (50-base field), "Built-in defaults".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "template",
          .aliases = "",
          .title = R"glossary(Template)glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** The DNA a primer anneals to, and that the polymerase
copies from the primer's 3' end on. In a PCR, both strands of the
target serve as templates, one for each primer.

**Method.** The template is the loaded molecule (a GenBank, FASTA or
plain sequence file), numbered from 1 on its top strand. A primer
written like the top strand (5' to 3') anneals to the complement
strand, and a "complement strand" primer anneals to the top strand.
Positions are top-strand coordinates of the primer's 5' end.

*Source:* README "Molecule file formats", "Find Pair".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "amplicon",
          .aliases = "product pcr-product",
          .title = R"glossary(Amplicon (PCR product))glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** The DNA fragment that a pair of primers amplifies: from
the 5' end of the forward primer to the 5' end of the reverse primer,
both primers included.

**Method.** For a pair made from a molecule (primer A on the top
strand, primer B on the complement strand), the product spans A's
position to B's position, both included (A at 51 and B at 400 give 350
bases); its length and GC content give the product Tm. How Primer
Designer counts 5' extensions in the product is not recorded.

*Source:* README "Product Tm and annealing temperature".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "extension",
          .aliases = "5'-extension 5-extension",
          .title = R"glossary(5' extension)glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** Extra bases added to the 5' end of a primer that do
not anneal to the template in the first PCR cycles, for instance a
restriction site or a tag. They become part of the product.

**Method.** The extension is part of the primer for every criterion
(%GC, runs, 3' G/C run, dimers, hairpins, and their drawings), except
the Tm, which is computed on the annealing part only. Reports print
the sequence as `5'-EXTENSION-PRIMER-3'`.

*Source:* README "5' extension and the criteria", "The One Primer report".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "gc",
          .aliases = "%gc gc-content",
          .title = R"glossary(%GC (GC content))glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** The percentage of G and C bases in a primer. G·C pairs
have three hydrogen bonds and stack more strongly than A·T pairs, so
GC-rich primers bind more stably.

**Method.**

    %GC = floor(100 × (number of G + number of C) / length)

The value is truncated to a whole percentage (9 G+C out of 17 bases:
52.9 %, shown 52). Criterion: within the range Min–Max, limits
included (default 50–60 %).

*Source:* README "Primer Tm", "Primer criteria".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "tm",
          .aliases = "melting-temperature",
          .title = R"glossary(Melting temperature (Tm) of a primer)glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** The temperature at which half of the primer molecules
are bound to their complementary sequence. It guides the choice of
the annealing temperature, and the two primers of a pair should have
similar Tm values.

**Method: classic Tm (v1 default).** A nearest-neighbour model: the
enthalpy ΔH (kcal/mol) and entropy ΔS (cal/K/mol) are sums over the
primer's consecutive base pairs (dinucleotide steps), with the
parameters of Breslauer et al. (1986), then

    Tm (°C) = floor( 1000 × ΔH / (ΔS + 60.69987) − 273 )

- no initiation, terminal or symmetry term; no salt or concentration
  term: 60.69987 cal/K/mol is a constant fitted on 200 primers recorded
  from Primer Designer (any value above 60.699732 and up to 60.7 fits;
  60.7 is probably the original value, the middle of the interval
  avoids rounding ties). Its origin is not known; it equals Primer3's
  formula with a primer concentration of about 50 pM;
- the offset is 273, not 273.15, and the result is truncated (floor);
- a Tm at or below 0 °C is shown as "?" (0 on the analysis screen);
- the 5' extension is not included.

Breslauer et al. (1986), Table 2 (ΔH kcal/mol, ΔS cal/K/mol; a step
and its complement share their values, e.g. AA = TT):

| Step | ΔH | ΔS | Step | ΔH | ΔS |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| AA/TT | 9.1 | 24.0 | CA/TG | 5.8 | 12.9 |
| AT | 8.6 | 23.9 | GT/AC | 6.5 | 17.3 |
| TA | 6.0 | 16.9 | CT/AG | 7.8 | 20.8 |
| GA/TC | 5.6 | 13.5 | CG | 11.9 | 27.8 |
| GC | 11.1 | 26.7 | GG/CC | 11.0 | 26.6 |

Criterion: within Min–Max, limits included (default 55–80 °C).

*Alternatives (planned for v2):* SantaLucia (1998) unified
nearest-neighbour parameters, with salt corrections (SantaLucia 1998,
Owczarzy et al. 2004) and Mg²⁺ and dNTP corrections (Owczarzy et al.
2008, von Ahsen et al. 2001).

*References:* Breslauer KJ, Frank R, Blöcker H, Marky LA (1986)
Predicting DNA duplex stability from the base sequence. PNAS 83:
3746-3750. *Source:* README "Primer Tm", "Primer Tm: the constant b",
"Primer3 equivalent".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "product-tm",
          .aliases = "",
          .title = R"glossary(Product Tm)glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** The melting temperature of the double-stranded PCR
product (amplicon). It depends mostly on the product's GC content and
length.

**Method: classic product Tm (v1 default).** The product spans primer
A's 5' end to primer B's 5' end (both included).

    product Tm (°C) = floor( 59.9 + 41 × GC fraction − 675 / length )

that is 81.5 + 16.6 log10(0.05) + 0.41 × %GC − 675 / length, a
classical empirical formula for 50 mM monovalent salt. The GC fraction
is not rounded.

*References (proposed, to verify):* Rychlik W, Spencer WJ, Rhoads RE
(1990) Nucleic Acids Research 18:6409-6412, which uses the product Tm
in its annealing temperature (see "Annealing temperature"); the
salt term goes back to Schildkraut C, Lifson S (1965) Dependence of
the melting temperature of DNA on salt concentration. Biopolymers
3:195-208. Not checked against the paper's full text yet (the scanned
PDF could not be retrieved). *Source:* README "Product Tm and annealing
temperature".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "annealing-temperature",
          .aliases = "ta",
          .title = R"glossary(Annealing temperature (Ta))glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** The temperature of the annealing step of a PCR cycle,
at which primers bind to the template. Too low: primers bind
unspecifically; too high: little product.

**Method: classic annealing temperature (v1 default),** after Rychlik
et al. (1990), from the displayed (truncated) Tm values:

    Ta (°C) = floor( 0.3 × (Tm of the primer with the lower Tm − 21.6)
                     + 0.7 × product Tm − 14.9 )

21.6 = −16.6 log10(0.05): the primer Tm is brought to the same 50 mM
salt basis as the product Tm.

*References:* Rychlik W, Spencer WJ, Rhoads RE (1990) Optimization of
the annealing temperature for DNA amplification in vitro. Nucleic
Acids Research 18:6409-6412. *Source:* README "Product Tm and annealing
temperature".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "hairpin",
          .aliases = "hairpins",
          .title = R"glossary(Hairpin)glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** A structure in which a primer folds back on itself: two
complementary stretches of the same primer pair into a stem, closed by
an unpaired loop. A stable hairpin competes with annealing to the
template, especially when it involves the 3' end.

**Method: classic hairpin energy (v1 default).** The most stable
hairpin is searched and its free energy estimated (kcal/mol, at
37 °C):

    energy = loop penalty − sum of stacking stabilities along the stem

- stems are runs of contiguous pairs; G·T pairs are allowed inside a
  stem, never at its ends; a stem is zipped inwards as long as the next
  pair is a Watson-Crick pair and the loop keeps at least 3 bases;
- stacking stabilities: Freier et al. (1986), RNA nearest-neighbour
  values read for DNA (T for U), kcal/mol: AA 0.9, AT 0.9, TA 1.1,
  CT 1.7, CA 1.8, AC 2.1, GA 2.3, CG 2.0, GG 2.9, GC 3.4 (and their
  complements); steps with a G·T pair have their own values;
- loop penalty, Freier et al. (1986), by loop length: 3: 7.4,
  4: 5.9, 5: 4.4, 6: 4.3, 7: 4.1, 8: 4.1, 9: 4.2, 10: 4.3, 12: 4.9,
  14: 5.6, 16: 6.1, 18: 6.7, 20: 7.1, 30: 8.9; linear interpolation in
  between, and beyond 30 the slope from 20 to 30 (0.18 per base);
- only a hairpin with a negative energy is reported; the energy is
  shown truncated toward zero to one decimal (−2.16 is shown −2.1).

Note: hairpins use Freier's RNA parameters, while the primer Tm uses
Breslauer's DNA parameters (as Primer Designer does).

Criterion: a hairpin fails when its energy is below the cutoff
(default 0.0 kcal: any hairpin found fails).

*Alternatives (planned for v2):* thermodynamic hairpins (ΔG and
structure Tm with DNA parameters, as Primer3's `thal`).

*References:* Freier SM, Kierzek R, Jaeger JA, Sugimoto N, Caruthers
MH, Neilson T, Turner DH (1986) Improved free-energy parameters for
predictions of RNA duplex stability. PNAS 83:9373-9377. *Source:*
README "Primer criteria".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "dimer",
          .aliases = "dimers primer-dimer 3'-dimers",
          .title = R"glossary(Primer-dimer)glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** Two primer molecules annealing to each other through
complementary bases: two copies of the same primer (self-dimer: A:A,
B:B) or the two primers of a pair (A:B). If a primer's 3' end is
paired, the polymerase can extend it and make a short artefact
product that competes with the target.

**Method: classic dimer counts (v1 default).** The two primers are
aligned antiparallel in every possible offset. Two values are
reported:

- **3' dimers (matches at the 3' end):** the largest number of
  consecutive complementary pairs counted from a primer's 3' end, in
  alignments where the partner extends at least one base beyond that
  3' end (so that it can serve as a template for extension). For A:B,
  the larger of A's count on B and B's count on A. Criterion: reject
  if ≥ 3 (default).
- **Dimers, adjacent homologous bases (any dimers):** the longest run
  of consecutive complementary pairs anywhere, in any alignment.
  Criterion: reject if ≥ 7 (default).

Only Watson-Crick pairs count. Reports draw the duplex behind each
value ("Primer-dimers with 3'end involvement", "Other Primer-Dimers").

*Alternatives (planned for v2):* thermodynamic dimers (ΔG of the most
stable duplex, as Primer3's `thal`), instead of counting pairs.

*Source:* README "Primer criteria", "Primer pair analysis",
"Duplexes of the reports".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "runs",
          .aliases = "run",
          .title = R"glossary(Runs)glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** A run is a stretch of the same base repeated
(AAAA, GGGG). Long runs favour mispriming and polymerase slippage.

**Method.** The longest run of one base, *not counting* the run at the
primer's 3' end (a homopolymer gives 0; CAACTGCTATTT gives 2).
Criterion: reject if ≥ 3 (default). On the Primer Pair and Multi
Primer screens, a run is flagged "!" only from one more than the
criterion (4 with the default 3), as in Primer Designer. Find Pair, however, also rejects
a run at the 3' end that reaches the criterion (as Primer Designer).

*Source:* README "Primer criteria", "Primer pair analysis", "Find Pair
and runs at the 3' end".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "gc-clamp",
          .aliases = "3'-gc-run gc-run",
          .title = R"glossary(3' G/C run (GC clamp))glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** The G and C bases at a primer's 3' end. One or two
help the 3' end bind firmly (a "GC clamp"); too many make the 3' end
bind stably to wrong sites.

**Method: classic 3' G/C run (v1 default).** The number of
consecutive G or C bases at the 3' end. Criterion: reject if ≥ 3
(default); on the pair screens, flagged from 4, as for runs.

*Alternatives (planned for v2):* 3'-end stability (ΔG of the last five
bases).

*Source:* README "Primer criteria".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "criteria",
          .aliases = "meets-criteria",
          .title = R"glossary(Criteria and "Meets criteria")glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** The checks a primer must pass to be proposed: %GC,
Tm, hairpins, 3' dimers, dimers, runs, 3' G/C runs.

**Method.** %GC and Tm pass inside their ranges, limits included; the
four counts fail when they reach their "reject if ≥" value; the
hairpin check fails when a hairpin is below the energy cutoff.
"Meets criteria" is YES when all seven pass. Values are the built-in
defaults, or those of a settings file (`pd2.cfg`, option `--config` of
the command line and of the graphical interface); a primer retrieved
from a `.PD2` file keeps its own. (Modify → Criteria is not available
yet.)

*Source:* README "Primer criteria", "Built-in defaults".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "balance",
          .aliases = "primer-balance pair",
          .title = R"glossary(Primer balance (pair))glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** How well the two primers of a pair match each other:
both should anneal under the same conditions.

**Method.** Differences in length, %GC and Tm of A and B (absolute
values of the displayed numbers). Searches for a partner (Match Active
Primer, Find Pair) keep pairs whose %GC differ by at most the GC
tolerance (default 5 %) and Tm by at most the Tm tolerance (default
10 °C).

*Source:* README "Primer pair analysis", "Match Active Primer".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "uniqueness",
          .aliases = "homology",
          .title = R"glossary(Uniqueness)glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** Whether a primer binds at only one place on the
template. Other places with long stretches identical to the primer
are "regions of homology".

**Method.** Both strands of the molecule are searched for maximal
exact matches of 7 bases or more between a stretch of the primer and
the molecule; the primer's own site is not listed, but copies of the
primer elsewhere are, full-length ones included. Each region shows
the matching bases, the molecule position facing their 5'-most base,
"(C)" on the complement strand, and the Tm of the matching bases
(primer Tm formula, truncated). Sites with Tm ≤ 0 are left out unless
the setting "exclude Tm ≤ 0" is N; regions are sorted by decreasing Tm
unless "sort by Tm" is N.

*Source:* README "Uniqueness and false priming", "The homology settings".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "false-priming",
          .aliases = "",
          .title = R"glossary(False priming)glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** Annealing of a primer's 3' end to an unintended site of
the template, where the polymerase can start synthesis and make
unwanted products.

**Method.** As uniqueness, but only matches of 6 bases or more that
include the primer's 3' end. Find Pair uses the highest false-priming
site Tm of each primer in its score.

*Source:* README "Uniqueness and false priming".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "match",
          .aliases = "match-active-primer",
          .title = R"glossary(Match Active Primer)glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** A search for a partner (primer B) for the current
primer (primer A) around a target position on the other strand.

**Method.** Primers B of the given length in the window target − start
to target + end that meet every criterion (with Find Pair's 3'-end run
rule), whose %GC and Tm are within
the tolerances of primer A's, and that have fewer A:B 3' dimers and
fewer A:B adjacent homologous bases than the criteria. Listed by
position, with their distance to the target.

*Source:* README "Match Active Primer", "The Match Active Primer report".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "find-pair",
          .aliases = "score",
          .title = R"glossary(Find Pair and its score)glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** A search for the best pairs of primers around two
targets: primer A on the top strand before target A, primer B on the
complement strand after target B.

**Method.** Possible primers: those of each window that meet every
criterion and whose run at the 3' end is shorter than the runs
criterion (unlike the Primer Analysis, which does not count that run).
Possible pairs: %GC and Tm within the tolerances, fewer A:B 3' dimers
and fewer A:B adjacent homologous bases than the criteria. Pairs are
ranked by increasing
**classic score (v1 default)** (equal scores keep the order in which
pairs were generated):

    score = wGC × |%GC A − %GC B| + wTm × |Tm A − Tm B|
          + w3' × max 3' dimers + wAny × max dimers
          + wFP × (false-priming Tm of A + false-priming Tm of B)

with weights from 0 (ignore) to 5 (default 1 each); "max" is the
largest of A:A, B:B and A:B; a primer without false-priming site
counts 0. Targets are checked first: target A from 1 to the molecule's
length, with target A + primer length within the molecule, and target
B from the primer length to the molecule's length ("Must enter value
for this field", "Primer too long to fit within molecule."). Limits: a
window with 100 possible primers or more stops the search; at 1000
pairs, the best 1000 of the first 1001 pairs are kept (primerpicker
then warns, where Primer Designer may hang).

*Alternatives (planned for v2):* Primer3-compatible automatic pair
picking.

*Source:* README "Find Pair", "The false-priming term of the score",
"Limits on primers and pairs", "Targets near and beyond the molecule's
end", "Reports of more than 20 pairs", "Find Pair: A:B adjacent bases".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "restriction-sites",
          .aliases = "enzymes restriction-enzymes",
          .title = R"glossary(Restriction sites (true and almost sites))glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** A restriction enzyme cuts DNA at or near a specific
recognition sequence. Primers often carry such sites in a 5'
extension, for cloning.

**Method.** Both strands of the molecule are searched (across the
origin for a circular molecule). True sites match the recognition
sequence exactly; almost sites differ by at most one base (see "True
site and almost site"). A site is reported at the top-strand position of its
leftmost base; palindromic sites are reported once. Enzymes come from
REBASE (version 610, commercially available enzymes); old names such
as Asp718 or Sau3A are accepted.

*References:* Roberts RJ, Vincze T, Posfai J, Macelis D (2010) REBASE —
a database for DNA restriction and modification: enzymes, genes and
genomes. Nucleic Acids Research 38:D234-D236. doi:10.1093/nar/gkp874.
*Source:* README "Restriction enzymes".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "almost-site",
          .aliases = "true-site almost-sites true-sites",
          .title = R"glossary(True site and almost site)glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** A true site is an exact copy of an enzyme's recognition
sequence. An almost site differs from it by one base at most: one base
change (for instance through a mutagenic primer) turns it into a true
site, which is how new restriction sites are designed.

**Method.** Almost sites include the true sites. Each almost site
shows a dot for each matching base and the molecule's base where it
differs, read on the site's strand in top-strand order; a site on the
complement strand is compared with the reverse complement of the
recognition sequence (written in parentheses, or marked "C" for true
sites). A base matches an ambiguity letter (N, R, Y, ...) when it is
one of the bases the letter stands for. At the same position, exact
sites come before sites with a mismatch, then top before complement.

*Source:* README "True and almost sites".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "circular",
          .aliases = "circular-molecule plasmid",
          .title = R"glossary(Circular molecule)glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** A molecule whose two ends are joined, such as a plasmid
or many viral, bacterial and organelle genomes. A site or a primer can
then span the junction between the last and the first base (the
origin of the numbering).

**Method.** A molecule is circular when the LOCUS line of its GenBank
file says so; FASTA and plain sequence files are linear. In v1, only
the restriction site search uses it: sites across the origin are
found, and reported at the top-strand position of their leftmost base
(an AluI site split over bases 599, 600, 1 and 2 of a 600-base
molecule is reported at 599). Everywhere else, as in Primer Designer,
a circular molecule is treated as linear: primers cannot cross the
origin ("Invalid Position"), Find Pair windows stop at the molecule's
ends, and uniqueness and false priming do not find sites across the
origin.

*Source:* README "Molecule file formats", "True and almost sites",
"Circular molecules".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "multi-primer",
          .aliases = "multi",
          .title = R"glossary(Multi Primer Analysis)glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** A side-by-side check of two to four primers meant to
be used together (multiplex PCR, nested primers, sequencing primers).

**Method.** For each primer: length, %GC, Tm, runs, 3' G/C runs,
hairpins; for every pair of primers (and each primer with itself): 3'
dimers and dimers, as in the Primer Pair analysis.

*Source:* README "Multi Primer Analysis".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "nested-primers",
          .aliases = "nested",
          .title = R"glossary(Nested primers)glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** A second pair of primers that anneals inside the
product of a first (outer) pair. In nested PCR, a first reaction with
the outer primers is followed by a second one, on its product, with
the inner (nested) primers: unspecific products of the first reaction
rarely contain sites for the inner primers, so the second reaction is
more specific and more sensitive. In semi-nested PCR, one outer primer
is kept.

**Method.** primerpicker, as Primer Designer, has no dedicated
nested-primer search (none is planned). Nested primers are designed as two pairs: the
outer pair (Find Pair, or Match Active Primer), then the inner pair
with targets inside the outer product; Multi Primer Analysis then
checks up to four primers together, outer and inner, for 3' dimers and
dimers between every pair of them. Each inner primer should also
anneal well below the outer product's ends (check its position against
the outer pair's product).

*References:* none yet. *Source:* README "Multi Primer Analysis",
"Find Pair", "Match Active Primer".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "sequencing-primer",
          .aliases = "sequencing",
          .title = R"glossary(Sequencing primer)glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** A single primer that starts a sequencing reaction (for
instance Sanger sequencing) at a chosen place: the read extends from
its 3' end, so the primer is placed upstream of the region to read, on
the strand to read. The first bases after the primer are usually not
readable, so it is placed some distance before the region of interest.

**Method.** primerpicker v1, as Primer Designer, applies the same
single-primer criteria to sequencing and PCR primers (Primer
Analysis, uniqueness, false priming); it has no criterion specific to
sequencing. Uniqueness matters most: a sequencing primer must anneal
at one place only.

*Source:* README "Primer criteria", "Uniqueness and false priming".
)glossary"},
    Entry{.key = "mutagenize",
          .aliases = "mutagenesis",
          .title = R"glossary(Mutagenize)glossary",
          .text = R"glossary(**Definition.** Editing a primer to introduce a mutation (a base change
that creates a restriction site, or changes an amino acid), while
checking the effect on the protein and on the primer's criteria.

**Method.** The molecule and the edited primer are translated in the
three reading frames of the top strand with the standard genetic code
(stop codons as "-"); the original and mutated primers' %GC, Tm,
hairpin, dimers and runs are compared.

*Source:* README "Mutagenize".
)glossary"},
}};

}  // namespace primerpicker::glossary
